Here's a complete example. I'll walk through each file and explain how DI works.

---

### Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts for a given user.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUserPosts(
        [Description("The numeric user ID whose posts to retrieve")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching posts for user {UserId}", userId);
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/{userId}/posts", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }
}
```

---

### `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();   // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();        // discovers all [McpServerToolType] classes

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class (shown above) | Services in the constructor, tool params on the method |
| **Method injection** | Static class | DI services as method parameters alongside tool params |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes tool parameters from injected services. Types registered in the DI container (`HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `IHttpClientFactory`, etc.) are resolved at call time and **never appear** in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

Built-in injectable types (no registration needed):
- `CancellationToken` — cooperative cancellation
- `IMcpServer` — server instance for notifications
- `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` — progress reporting
- `ILogger<T>` — logging (registered by default host)

You register additional services (`AddHttpClient()`, `AddSingleton<T>()`, etc.) in `Program.cs`, and the SDK injects them automatically.
